<title>USA: U.S. May import prices unchanged.</title>
<headline>U.S. May import prices unchanged.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Department report of U.S. import and export price changes by percent (unadjusted).</p>
<p>    -			    May    Apr-Rev from  May 97/96</p>
<p> ALL IMPORTS		  Unch   -1.2   -0.9     -3.0</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    1.2   -7.0   -3.8     -9.2</p>
<p> -Nonpetroleum		-0.1   -0.6   -0.6     -2.4</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	  1.7   -1.8   -1.6	0.2</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies	0.4   -2.9   -1.6     -3.9</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.8   -1.1   -1.1     -6.2</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    -0.2   -0.2   -0.2     -0.1</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos   0.2   Unch   Unch     -0.8</p>
<p>Percent changes, not seasonally adjusted --</p>
<p> ALL EXPORTS		  -0.2   -0.7   -0.6     -2.8</p>
<p> -Agricultural		-1.1   -6.9   -6.7    -20.0</p>
<p> -Non-Agricultural	  -0.1    0.1    0.1     -0.1</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	 -1.1   -7.4   -7.2    -21.8</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies     -0.1   -0.1   Unch     -0.5</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.1   -0.1   Unch     -0.5</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    -0.1    0.3    0.3	1.0</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos   0.1    0.1    0.1	1.0</p>
<p>The department reported May price indexes (1995 base year equals 100) of 98.2 for imports, and 98.8 for exports.</p>
<p>The 0.1 percent decline in nonpetroleum import prices was the fifth consecutive monthly drop, the department said.</p>
</text>
